Article 19
(1) The Regional Government prevents the occurrence of violence against women and children, carries out empowerment and awareness-raising for families, parents and the community by providing information, guidance and/or outreach.
(2) In addition to empowerment and awareness-raising for families, parents and the community by providing information, guidance and/or outreach as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regional Government also undertakes efforts as follows:
a. increasing the quantity and quality of education, both formal and non-formal and informal;
b. opening access to obtain education, training, funding, income enhancement and social services;
c. opening employment opportunities for women;
d. building community participation and concern to implement prevention and protection of women and children from violence;
e. building and providing a complete and easily accessible information system;
f. building networks and cooperation with law enforcement apparatus, government apparatus, universities and various non-governmental organizations that work and/or care for women and children; and
g. opening complaint posts for the protection of women and children from violence.

Article 5
Protection as referred to in Article 4 includes: a. prevention of violence against women and children; b. provision of integrated services and assistance for women and children victims of violence;
c. strengthening and development of institutions providing protection services for women and children.

Article 20
(1) The Regional Government prevents the occurrence of violence against women and children as referred to in Article 19 paragraph (1), which is carried out by Regional Apparatus that have duties and functions in the fields of:
a. social;
b. health;
c. education;
d. employment;
e. women's empowerment and child protection;
f. mental and spiritual; and
g. tranquility and public order.
(2) Prevention of violence by regional apparatus as referred to in paragraph (1) is implemented in an integrated and continuous manner based on the Regional Action Plan.

Article 12
(1) The community has obligations and responsibilities in the implementation of protection for women and children victims of violence as referred to in Article 10 letter b which is carried out in the form of community participation.
(2) Forms of community participation as referred to in paragraph (1) include:
a. establishing family partners at the kelurahan level;
b. establishing women and children protection units within community organizations;
c. conducting independent socialization of women's and children's rights;
d. providing first aid to victims; and
e. reporting to the competent authorities if violence against victims occurs in their environment.

Article 3
(1) The implementation of Protection of Women and Children from acts of violence aims to:
a. protect and provide a sense of security for women and children;
b. provide services to victims of violence, reporters and witnesses;
c. empower women and children who are victims of acts of violence.
(2) The objectives of implementing protection of women and children as referred to in paragraph (1) include:
a. prevention;
b. risk reduction; and
c. handling

CHAPTER II
PRINCIPLES AND OBJECTIVES
Article 2
Protection of Women and Children from acts of violence is implemented based on the principles of:
a. non-discrimination;
b. best interests of women and children;
c. justice and gender equality;
d. respect for the opinion of the child;
e. respect for the rights of victims;
f. legal certainty; and
g. local wisdom.

Article 11
(1) Regional Governments have the obligation and responsibility to implement protection efforts for women and children victims of violence through:
a. implementing protection policies for women and children victims of violence as applied by the government;
b. establishing policies, programs, and activities for the protection of women and children victims of violence;
c. establishment of P2TP2A;
d. ensuring the implementation of service facilitation for victims;
e. pursuing effectiveness and efficiency for the victim recovery process; and
f. pursuing the creation of cooperation and coordination in victim recovery efforts.
(2) Regional Governments in carrying out the obligations and responsibilities as referred to in paragraph (1) letter b, shall establish action programs and activities for the protection of women and children in 1 (one) Regional Action Plan.
(3) The Regional Action Plan as referred to in paragraph (2) shall serve as the basis for Regional Government Apparatus in implementing protection for women and children victims of violence.
(4) The Regional Action Plan as referred to in paragraph (2) constitutes part of the RPJMD.
(5) The Regional Action Plan as referred to in paragraph (2) shall be further regulated in Mayor Regulation.

Article 7
(1) Physical Violence as referred to in Article 6 letter a is an act that results in pain, injury, wounds, or disability to a person's body, miscarriage, fainting, and that causes death.
(2) Sexual Violence as referred to in Article 6 letter b is an act due to:
a. sexual harassment;
b. coercion of unnatural or unwanted sexual relations; and
c. coercion of sexual relations with others for commercial purposes or certain purposes.
(3) Economic Violence as referred to in Article 6 letter c is an act that restricts the wife from working inside or outside the home to earn money and goods, including allowing the wife who works to be exploited, while the husband does not fulfill the family's economic needs. Economic violence can also take the form of:
a. a husband who does not provide maintenance to his wife because his wife has an income;
b. a husband concealing his income;
c. taking the wife's assets;
d. not providing sufficient household money or not providing household money at all;
e. demanding the wife obtain more income; and
f. not permitting the wife to advance her career.
(4) Social Violence as referred to in Article 6 letter d is an act by a person or group of people that causes injury or loss of life to a person or can cause physical damage or damage to another person's property/goods;
(5) Psychological Violence as referred to in Article 6 letter e is an act that results in fear, loss of self-confidence, loss of ability to act, feelings of helplessness and/or severe psychological suffering to a person.
(6) Household neglect as referred to in Article 6 letter f is caused by:
a. an act that results in the failure to fulfill a child's needs properly, whether physical, mental, spiritual, social, committed by parents, guardians, or other parties responsible for their care;
b. an act of deliberately neglecting to maintain, care for, or look after a child as appropriate committed by parents, guardians or any other party responsible for their care;
c. an act of neglecting a person within the scope of their household, whereas according to the law applicable to them or due to an agreement or contract they are obligated to provide livelihood, care, or maintenance to that person; and/or
d. an act that results in economic dependence by means of restricting and/or prohibiting proper work inside or outside the home so that the victim is under the control of that person.
(7) Exploitation as referred to in Article 6 letter g is caused because of:
a. an act that exploits economically or sexually with the intention of benefiting oneself or others;
b. an act with or without the victim's consent that includes but is not limited to prostitution, forced labor or service, slavery or similar practices, oppression, extortion, physical exploitation, exploitation of sexual organs, reproductive organs, or unlawfully transferring or transplanting organs and/or body tissues, and/or utilizing the labor or ability of a person by another party to obtain benefits both material and immaterial; and/or
c. any form of utilization of the victim's sexual organs or other body organs to obtain benefits, including but not limited to all activities of prostitution or indecency/sexual abuse.

Article 14
(1) The Regional Government provides services and protection to women and children from violence by establishing P2TP2A as an integrated service center for women and children victims of violence.
(2) P2TP2A as referred to in paragraph (1), shall be established by Decree of the Mayor.
(3) P2TP2A as referred to in paragraph (2), consists of elements:
a. Health Service;
b. Education Service;
c. DINSOSP2KBP3A;
d. Trade, Cooperatives, Small and Medium Enterprises, and Industry Service;
e. Population and Civil Registration Service;
f. Civil Service Police Unit and Fire Department;
g. Regional General Hospital;
h. City Resort Police (Kepolisian Resor Kota);
i. Non-Governmental Organizations; and
j. universities.
(4) P2TP2A as referred to in paragraph (2) shall provide services to victims of violence in an integrated manner.
(5) P2TP2A as referred to in paragraph (4) may receive and send case referrals from or to other service units in a networked manner.
(6) P2TP2A provides temporary services and protection in the form of a safe house.
(7) In the event that P2TP2A does not yet have a safe house, then P2TP2A may refer victims of violence to P2TP2A that has a safe house.
(8) P2TP2A has a counseling field consisting of at least:
a. legal field;
b. health field;
c. spiritual field; and
d. social field.
(9) Further provisions regarding the organizational structure of P2TP2A shall be regulated in the Mayor's Regulation.

Article 23
(1) Forms of services provided to women and children victims of violence are implemented as follows:
a. complaint services;
b. health services;
c. legal aid;
d. return;
e. rehabilitation, social reintegration, and medicolegal;
f. identification services; and
g. psychological services.
(2) Forms of services as referred to in paragraph (1) are implemented in accordance with minimum service standards established by the Regional Government and implemented by regional apparatus having duties and functions in the fields of:
a. social affairs;
b. health;
c. women's empowerment and child protection; and
d. mental and spiritual.
(3) In carrying out duties and functions as referred to in paragraph (2), the Regional Government cooperates with Government agencies, the Provincial Government, other Regency/City Governments, the community, families and parents.
(4) Further provisions regarding procedures for services and handling of women and children victims of violence as referred to in paragraph (1), paragraph (2) and paragraph (3) shall be regulated in a Mayor's Regulation.

